<!-- ─── SECTION 6: RECOMMENDATIONS ─── -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-title">6. Strategic Recommendations</div>
  <p class="section-intro">
    Every recommendation below is grounded in a specific finding from the data. No extrapolation beyond what the dataset directly supports.
  </p>

  <div class="rec-grid">
    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>1. Price Fiction Below $12</h4>
      <p>Fiction bestsellers average $10.85, yet outperform Non Fiction in both average rating (4.65 vs 4.60) and review volume (15,684 vs 9,065). The $6–10 price band is the single most-populated segment (179 books). Fiction titles priced above $15 face competitive headwinds from better-rated, lower-priced alternatives already on the list.</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: Genre comparison table; Price segment analysis ($6–10 avg rating 4.65)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>2. Don't Chase Ratings via Higher Prices</h4>
      <p>The Pearson correlation of r = −0.133 confirms that raising price does not produce higher ratings. The $0–5 segment earns the highest avg rating (4.72). Any premium-pricing rationale based on quality signaling is not supported by customer behavior in this dataset — price discipline is a competitive advantage, not a concession.</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: Pearson coefficient; Price segment avg ratings</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>3. Invest in Series-Driven Fiction Authors</h4>
      <p>The three Fiction authors with the most appearances (Kinney 12, Riordan 11, Collins 11) are all series writers. Series create recurring bestseller appearances without requiring new audience acquisition. A single series entry can produce 3–5 distinct bestseller slots across multiple years. Prioritize series acquisition and development in the Fiction catalog.</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: Top-10 authors table; all three Fiction leaders are series authors</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>4. Monitor Non Fiction Pricing Post-2015</h4>
      <p>Non Fiction prices dropped from a peak of ~$20.81 (2014, genre avg) to ~$10.57 (2019). This 49% decline in 5 years signals structural market repricing — not a temporary dip. Publishers relying on premium Non Fiction margins need a revised pricing model anchored to the new market equilibrium below $15.</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: Year-over-year by genre table (NF price 2014 vs 2019)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>5. Prioritize High-Volume Review Books</h4>
      <p>Average review count grew 3.4× from 2009 to 2014 and stabilized at ~14,000–16,000 per year through 2019. Books with very high review counts (>20K) appear repeatedly — they benefit from Amazon's ranking algorithms. Editorial strategy should favor titles with demonstrated review-generation potential (strong communities, adaptation potential, educational curricula).</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: Year-over-year avg reviews; recurring titles like Oh, the Places You'll Go! (21,834 reviews, 8 appearances)</div>
    </div>

    <div class="rec-card">
      <h4>6. Flag the "High-Price Exception" Category</h4>
      <p>Institutional/reference works (DSM-5 at $105, APA Manual at $46) sustain bestseller status through inelastic, compulsory demand — not consumer preference. These should be managed as a separate pricing category from trade books. Their inclusion inflates Non Fiction's avg price by an estimated $2–3 and should be excluded from consumer pricing benchmarks.</p>
      <div class="rec-source">Source: APA (10 appearances, $46 avg); DSM-5 ($105, 2 appearances)</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

